{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/geist-the-sin-eaters/21454-sin-eaters-and-age","id":21454,"title":"Sin-eaters and Age","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Geist: The Sin-Eaters"],"posts":[{"date":"2013-12-01T22:22:21-08:00","id":21454,"title":"Sin-eaters and Age","content":"I just have a simple question. Do Sin-Eaters age? My players say that they do. But there are several references to Sin-eaters not aging in the books. Once again, any help would be appreciated.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Nothing is foolproof for a sufficiently talented fool.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/981-bigwhitt","name":"bigwhitt","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar981_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-01T23:22:12-08:00","id":21467,"title":"","content":"I believe their body is normal and even ages like normal. I remember there was talk about whether they should have any sort of longevity when they were designing them and they settled on just getting to finish your lifetime as suitable enough. But I believe their are magical methods for them to live longer, at the very least some of the Conspiracy level Krewes probably had Ceremonies to fend off death.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-01T23:26:57-08:00","id":21470,"title":"","content":"Sin-Eaters age, with the exception of founders of Conspiracies who've taken the Revenance Founding Benefit, who instead degrade in health with every decade past the age of a hundred.<br /> <br /> Ghosts are temporary, and emulating them tends to be similarly temporary. Everybody moves on eventually.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-02T00:02:07-08:00","id":21475,"title":"","content":"Sin-Eaters have been stated to not be able to return from a death of old age. This implies that Sin-Eaters can naturally reach old age.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-02T07:51:03-08:00","id":21623,"title":"","content":"The main reason I ask is that there was a part during the explanation of the Oracle Manifestation on page 138 of the Geist book that told about the Sin-Eater being buried in cement since the 1920's and waiting to break out, like torpor. Is this only because the Sin-eater was channeling Oracle Manifestation?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Nothing is foolproof for a sufficiently talented fool.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/981-bigwhitt","name":"bigwhitt","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar981_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-02T08:24:16-08:00","id":21639,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>bigwhitt</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/geist-the-sin-eaters/21454-sin-eaters-and-age?p=21623#post21623\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The main reason I ask is that there was a part during the explanation of the Oracle Manifestation on page 138 of the Geist book that told about the Sin-Eater being buried in cement since the 1920's and waiting to break out, like torpor. Is this only because the Sin-eater was channeling Oracle Manifestation?</div> </div> </div> </div>Being that the Bound in question is effectively in suspended animation, yes.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-06T09:48:51-08:00","id":24402,"title":"","content":"*slips in under the door*<br /> <br /> You know, I've frequently pondered that very same manifestation-use, bigwhitt. And, *a polite tip of his hat to Satchel* I have concluded the suspended animation as an assumption myself, frequently. I do wonder though, as an opposing, (though by no means argumentative) point, considering the fact that they do age, that perhaps the body withers as/if they reach a point past their normal lifespans. After all, it does say in some of the flim-flam wherein, that only damage done to the body beforehand is healed upon the wakening of said Sin Eater; and no damage taken thereafter is considered. <br /> <br /> In this point, if we considered aging to be a damaging effect, (&quot;Ravages of Time&quot;, as it were.) which is a constant variable (that invariably leads all creatures not inherently Immortal into death anyhow.) then no, you would not be protected by the power. And as an aside, under &quot;Success&quot; in the Elemental Oracle, you can read that the person falls into a &quot;death-like coma&quot;. Coma, not stasis or suspended animation, necessarily. And people in coma's can die of old age. *he does hold up one of his fongs; an appendage much like a hand without fingers, and surprisingly animate and dexterous nonetheless: to forestall words momentarily* But that is merely myself lending to the Devil's Advocate, which I am licensed to freely exercise, by-the-by. *And displays the license for those that would dispute his claim: it looks as though he will need to renew it soon.*<br /> <br /> I tend to agree with dear Satchel though; more on wishful thinking and my own preference than anything purely logical. It's just fun to think of it that way anyhow. The wording in both versions of Geist has troubled me. A body in a coma would eventually die of old age; and a body would rot regardless, as time passed. <br /> <br /> In games I have ran, I often go with the suspended animation line of thought. *Another nod to Satchel* For simplicity sake.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> *beams and brief smile, and looks for an &quot;h&quot; to get comfortable on*","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1219-shake","name":"Shake","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1219_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"20"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-22T21:22:29-08:00","id":35359,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>bigwhitt</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/geist-the-sin-eaters/21454-sin-eaters-and-age?p=21623#post21623\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The main reason I ask is that there was a part during the explanation of the Oracle Manifestation on page 138 of the Geist book that told about the Sin-Eater being buried in cement since the 1920's and waiting to break out, like torpor. Is this only because the Sin-eater was channeling Oracle Manifestation?</div> </div> </div> </div>Key words on p.138 are &quot;some legends speak of a Sin-Eater who ran afoul of the Mob in the 1920s&quot;. A lot of Sin-eater culture is made up myths/facts/comic books and anything else a Sin-eater fancies. What I guess I am trying to say is it's nothing more than an urban legend. Definitely not concrete proven fact...<br /> <br /> The character is not is suspended animation but in a coma like state... Any damage the character taken prior to successfully entering the is healed once it ends.<br /> <br /> At the fourth dot of this power there is no time limit other than that of the character's Plasm as outlined by the cost to maintain this ability.<br /> <br /> What I find a little confusing is when the ability ends whatever the character used to induce Oracle, he or she is free of. Wouldn't that mean in the example of being buried alive as a result of a cave in, would the character just reappear somewhere safe whether above the cave in or on either side of the cave in?<br /> <br /> Further using the cave in as an example while the character escapes the crushing death from being buried alive, wouldn't the character still take damage from suffocating? If it was in a mine or cavern what are the chances of water complicating this (if the story dictates)? P.138 states outside damage to the body from an unrelated source would still in fact damage the character.<br /> <br /> The Geist's ability to Resurrect the Sin-eater brings the character back from death. It doesn't state where the character is resurrected just when. The Chapter intro from p 214 the protagonist awakens from death in a place other from where he died. The protagonist is killed in a cemetery and awakens in a crypt. How he got there is left to our imagination.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/573-darck-child","name":"Darck Child","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-23T09:38:49-08:00","id":35634,"title":"","content":"*A slight nod at D.C.*<br /> <br /> As usual, White Wolf lets our imaginations fill in the myriad gaps; purposely I wager. All the more reason they go about making many parts of their books vague: they don't want their rules taken to Court, as it were, and fought between Rule-Lawyers. *A slight smile, full of mirth* As always, it's up to the interpertation of the Storyteller to dictate how it's played in the game. I imagine you can play it any which way you like. Using your example of a cave-in, we can assume that all those variables are only relevant if someone is going to come and actually rescue the Sin Eater in question. If he doesn't have a Krewe, or anyone nearby wherein he can target for his abilities, I would think it would be safe to assume that he/she runs out of their stores of various traits, and then eventually withers. (Or their Geist sees how hopeless the situation is, and abandons them: a likelier scenario, whether it's exactly true or not. Geists can be fickle.)<br /> <br /> Opposingly, if there were such hope, it would more or less be according to the situation. Grave-dirt does generally say that if you are buried alive, that once you return to your body, you are more or less shot to the surface, with a spray of dirt going all around; though I would place my bet that they just used this as an example, and only expected characters to be buried alive in a cemetary, rather than in a cave-in. (i know they used that as an example...) Again, I generally defer to my ST; or if I'm in his place, I rule what's more interesting to the story, and my players; and then I stick to that ruling in the future.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1219-shake","name":"Shake","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1219_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"20"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-03T17:37:08-08:00","id":42060,"title":"","content":"I tend to err on the side of Rule of Cool, and the idea of a Sin-Eater wandering around disconnected from his body, which is in the concrete of a foundation of a major building, unable to make contact, slowly going insane, appeals to me as a narrative.<br /> <br /> As Shake says, I'd prefer that they leave it vague, much for fun that way. I mean, you can always change the rules, but not defining it leads to less arguments since it's well in the realm of ST-fiat.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-11T23:54:13-08:00","id":47272,"title":"","content":"I agree with Satchel and the majestic posting ninja Shake, but I would also like to bring another little thing up. While not expressly stated to the point of being a mechanical system, it is alluded to several times in the core book and couple times in Book of The Dead that while they are not immortal and still age, Sin-Eaters who are not repeatedly killed do tend to live impressively long lives. Not like that of a Changeling with high Wyrd, but it is (vaguely) said that they typically have something of an extended lifespan, though it is nebulous in that regard. There is a story hook in the core about an aging Bound forestalling death by living the bottled memories of ghosts, during which time his body did not age, acting as a hinted method of how players might be able to stave off death by old age. So while it is true that unless they discover a ceremony or a sort of secret or power in the Underworld that reverses, slows, or stops aging, they will eventually grow old and die. But at the same time, it isn't a bad thing, and I for one enjoy a bookend that has the character passing on after living out the life that his very determination served to preserve through calling out to his Geist. And it is unlikely that the Geist is going to be gone for good, allowing for the player to still hang onto that character in spirit, so all in all I am content.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1686,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","user":"Korogra","date":". 01-12-2014, 02:57 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","name":"Korogra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1686_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1310"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-12T09:13:45-08:00","id":47422,"title":"","content":"The thing is, infinitesimally few people really die of &quot;old age&quot;. What happens is that their bodily systems for healing injury, fighting disease, and processing toxic substances slow down enough for <i>something else</i> to kill them. Usually it's disease, or cancer, or a bad fall, or cholesterol buildup around the heart. None of those things are &quot;old age&quot;, and they also all fall into the category of &quot;things that you're basically immune to as a sin-eater&quot;. We don't actually know how long the average person can live before their bodily systems just fail under their own power, without some pathogen or circumstance hastening it. So it's reasonable to assume that even with no immunity to age, a sin-eater could live for what is (by human standards) a really, really long time. They just won't necessarily be the picture of health and fitness while they do it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":996,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/996-gabby","user":"Gabby","date":". 01-12-2014, 12:22 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/996-gabby","name":"Gabby","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-12T10:50:43-08:00","id":47479,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gabby</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/geist-the-sin-eaters/21454-sin-eaters-and-age?p=47422#post47422\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The thing is, infinitesimally few people really die of &quot;old age&quot;. What happens is that their bodily systems for healing injury, fighting disease, and processing toxic substances slow down enough for <i>something else</i> to kill them. Usually it's disease, or cancer, or a bad fall, or cholesterol buildup around the heart. None of those things are &quot;old age&quot;, and they also all fall into the category of &quot;things that you're basically immune to as a sin-eater&quot;. We don't actually know how long the average person can live before their bodily systems just fail under their own power, without some pathogen or circumstance hastening it. So it's reasonable to assume that even with no immunity to age, a sin-eater could live for what is (by human standards) a really, really long time. They just won't necessarily be the picture of health and fitness while they do it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agreed, though thanks to plasm they probably be much &quot;healthier&quot; than an ordinary person who is the same age, assuming an baseline human can survive to it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1686-korogra","name":"Korogra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1686_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1310"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-12T12:36:01-08:00","id":47553,"title":"","content":"If I were going to model it, system-wise, I'd probably use the system for age-related deterioration from Revenance, except you can't reboot it by killing yourself, and when you run out of health points you die.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/996-gabby","name":"Gabby","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"226"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}